title the flag one host cannot test, and the ledger that reads itself back
prompt Kyri as Dream: read KYRI and GAUGE_STYLE, honor the ITINERARY inner loop -- roster cold then hot, reds first, QA on touch, the council rota, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first -- pick up the running thread from the newest recommend line, and work the higher Glow rune ports lane.
think The lap opened by the card's own order and the roster ran long -- its evidence file is stamped 20260826.234821 and I did not time the start, so no duration is claimed here -- and the reading was done before a byte moved: 70 of 71 green, tree_moved=no, and caravan_suite RED at caravan_harvest_witness. Reds-first books the allocation, so the width ratchet I had named as my next lap waited. The red was deterministic on a settled tree with no second session in the checkout, which is what made it worth believing where last night's three roster reds on the macOS bench were not.
think The failure was a build rather than a run, and the message named its own root: dependency on libc must be explicitly specified in the build command, at std.c.waitpid, referenced by sweep_one. Sound moved caravan/harvest.rye off std.os.linux.waitpid last night to close %282 -- correct, since a raw Linux syscall answers 9 to everything on a BSD kernel -- and std.c.waitpid is an extern "c" declaration rather than a syscall. rye build links no libc unless a caller passes -lc, and macOS links libc into every binary it builds, so the pier that made the change saw a clean build and this one saw none.
think The answer was already written in the tree, which is the part worth keeping: five witnesses in tools/ca/ already pass -lc -- read by grep -l, after I first wrote three from memory and had to correct it, which is %284's own lesson arriving one lap later -- because caravan/subscribe_poll_service.rye has reached c.nanosleep since before last night. The convention existed; the new caller was simply not joined to it. So the repair is one flag on one witness rather than a new abstraction, and the module's own door comment now says it, since a module that cannot be built without a flag should say so where a reader meets it.
think caravan/overtake.rye also builds harvest and was measured rather than assumed: it never references sweep_one, so Zig emits no extern and it needs no flag. That measurement is the difference between a repair and a sweep.
think Then the second red, found while reading the width work's own room. Four Glow witnesses cite REDS %278 for the rune_heads count, and %278 on this tree is the ASCII meter's character class. Reading each citation's subject against the headline of the row it names turned up nine such citations across two renumbers -- six from last night's %278-%280 to %283-%285, three from the earlier BSD-dialect re-seat to %271-%275. No standing guard reaches them and none could: every one names a row that exists.
think The same comparison, run mechanically over every headline in the pin and the shelves, found the sharper thing. %249/%271, %250/%272 and %251/%273 are three incidents standing twice -- identical headlines, identical stamps -- each booked on the macOS bench, shifted here by %252's repair, then re-seated a second time when it arrived again from xy. The spine reads 1..287 gapless and holds 284 distinct reds, and reds_ledger_monotone_scan.sh was GREEN through all of it, because it proves the numbers are whole rather than that the rows are distinct.
obs I did not book a third row for the double-book, and the reason is the finding itself. Its root is the number-allocation fault %230 and %252 already hold OPEN at Keaton's door; booking it again would be booking one red twice, which is precisely what the row measures. It rides in %287's body and on the card as that door's fourth firing.
obs The repair that does not reward the fault. A renumber tool would make renumbering cheaper, and the tree's own question -- name what the thing rewards -- says do not build one. What went in instead is a reading: one headline under two row numbers, deduped by (number, headline) so a closure note stays one row, held under a ceiling of three that only falls, proven both ways on planted ledgers. Three passes, a fourth refuses by name and prints which two numbers carry it.
obs Where the three BSD-family citations were repointed took a judgement worth recording. Both seatings stand in the spine, and the living tree already cites %249/%250 on ten lines carrying eleven mentions against %272/%274 on one line, so the repoint follows the majority: the tree speaks with one voice about that family even while two numbers stand for it.
obs Council rota N=3526 mod 5 = 1 -- Air, Saturn, law and boundary, whose sense is touch: press each boundary and feel whether the post stands. It read as the lap's own instruction twice. A build flag is a boundary the hand passes straight through on one host, and a row number is a boundary that means two different things on two piers. The row's fixed seat, Single-Stranded, asks you to check a part by trying to pull it out -- and pulling on caravan/harvest.rye brought a build flag with it.
obs Grade-what-you-touch, all at Meter and all A+ on the counted readings: the new shelf 100/100/100, reds_ledger_monotone_scan.sh 100/100/100 with 21 living citers, caravan/harvest.rye 100/100/100 with 8. construction/ITINERARY.md read Truth 80 on one unresolved path -- this log, cited before it existed -- and reads whole once it lands. No frame pushed.
obs The card carried a stale count and it fell on touch: 74 guards stand, and the split read 70 every-lap and 4 cadence where the roster's own records hold 3 cadence and 71 lap. The runner had been printing guards_run=71 the whole time. Repaired rather than booked -- a recited number that drifted is the ratchet the tree already names, and the %127 family already holds the lesson.
obs The round did not open with the twice-pulled rota's first gesture. git pull --rebase xy main is meant to run before any context is read, and I read the card first and pulled nothing. Measured at send time rather than assumed: git ls-remote showed xy and debrided both at 9f5439341812914a4622c26ca08284680af26e2d, the same commit this round opened on, so nothing was missed -- yet the discipline exists for the round where something is, and skipping it is worth writing down rather than excusing.
obs The standing adaptation grant went unspent again: no bash, sh, HTML or Python stood in the way of either red. The custody gates were not approached -- no key, no funds, no chain, no publish, no history rewrite, no collaborator surface. The seed cadence is Keaton's and was not touched.
obs I stopped and restarted the hot roster twice rather than let it measure a tree I was still correcting. Three numbers in this round's own prose were written from memory and read differently under grep -- five witnesses passing -lc where I had written three, ten lines carrying eleven mentions where I had written eleven sites, and a nineteen-minute roster I never timed. Each cost a roster run to fix honestly, and that is the right price: %284 booked exactly this fault one lap ago, and a round that repeats it while writing the row about it would be worth nothing.
obs Named and measured for the next lap rather than taken: glow/rune_shape.rye publishes usize in five struct fields and five accessors, while lower_shape.rye and lower_named_cast.rye already work entirely in u32 and pay @as(usize, @intCast(...)) at every call into it. Moving that boundary REMOVES casts, which is the opposite of the rishi/src/main.rye exemption and the reason it is worth doing. Corpus-wide, 394 of 677 single-name @as(usize, @intCast(x)) casts take an x the same file declares u32; 354 of those are unambiguous, the other 40 sitting in files that declare the name both ways.
